## CRIT

### R1-SAFE-CRIT-001 (= R1-CORR-MAJ-001 same defect, 2 angles): git diff missing -C $REPO_ROOT

**Bug:** scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 239 (or wherever the `git diff --unified=0 "${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD" -- "$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL"` lives) has NO `-C` flag. Git resolves pathspecs relative to CWD. From any non-root invocation, the repo-root-relative pathspec finds nothing → diff empty → branch_log_modified=0.

For blocked-on-adopter-conflict: silently PASSES the not-modified assertion even when log was actually changed (false-positive bypass).

For onboarded / onboarded-operator-bump: silently FAILS the modified assertion even when log was modified (false-negative — exit 1 with wrong reason).

All tests use `(cd "$repo_dir" && "$SCRIPT" ...)` from the repo root, masking the defect.

**Fix:** in scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh, change every `git diff` invocation that uses repo-relative pathspecs to include `-C "$REPO_ROOT"`. Specifically:

```bash
git -C "$REPO_ROOT" diff --unified=0 "${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD" -- "$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL"
```

Also check other `git diff` calls in the script — apply `-C "$REPO_ROOT"` consistently. Use grep to find all `git diff` calls.

Also check `git rev-parse` calls used for diff-base-vs-HEAD comparison (fix-round-7 added this); ensure `-C` is consistent.

**Test:** in tests/check_invocation_log/test_check_invocation_log_entry.sh, add a test case that invokes the script from a SUBDIRECTORY of the test repo (not the repo root). Use `(cd "$repo_dir/scripts" && "$SCRIPT" ...)` style. Fixtures: blocked-on-adopter-conflict + log MODIFIED in diff. Assert exit 1 + diagnostic 'log was modified for cascade-status=blocked-on-adopter-conflict' (NOT exit 0 — confirms the fix). Without the fix this would silently pass; with the fix it correctly catches the audit-trail violation.

## MAJ

### R1-CORR-MAJ-002: tmp.scaffold.* not gitignored

**Bug:** test_scaffold_downstream.sh sets `TMPDIR=${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX`. .gitignore has `tmp.*/` (added in fix-round-7) but tmp.scaffold.* matches that pattern only if the slash is treated as path-separator. Verify: `tmp.*/` matches any directory named `tmp.<anything>/`. `tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX/` matches → covered. **However** the reviewer claims it isn't covered. Verify the existing `.gitignore` pattern by running:

```bash
git check-ignore -v tmp.scaffold.test/ tmp.out.test/ tmp.repro.test/
```

If any of those show 'not ignored' → tighten .gitignore. Otherwise the reviewer's claim is wrong (false positive).

If .gitignore needs tightening: add explicit `tmp.scaffold.*/`, `tmp.out.*/`, `tmp.repro.*/` patterns alongside `tmp.*/`.

Also amend DISPATCH-NOTE if it says 'output dirs created under TMPDIR (NOT REPO_ROOT)' — actual posture is tmp dirs in REPO_ROOT due to macOS denylist; correct the spec language.

### R1-SAFE-MAJ-001: not-applicable + tooling-slice-id valid bypass remains

**Bug:** even with fix-round-8's --tooling-slice-id requirement, a cohort cascade operator (024C-F) can pass `--tooling-slice-id 024B-core` (valid ID, but not the slice they're in) along with `--allow-not-applicable` to bypass enforcement. The slice-id is operator-supplied; the script can't verify the operator is actually in the named slice.

**Disposition:** this is a workflow-side mitigation, deferred to 024B-extras. The 024B-extras CI workflow wiring will hardcode `--tooling-slice-id` per workflow file (cohort workflows pass NOTHING; tooling workflows pass the matching ID). Process-side: DISPATCH-NOTE author + R1 review catches.

**Fix:** add a comment block in scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh near the --tooling-slice-id parsing acknowledging this layered defense:
```bash
# NOTE: --tooling-slice-id is operator-supplied; it cannot detect operator misuse
# (e.g. cohort 024C operator passing --tooling-slice-id 024B-core). The intended
# layered defense is workflow-side: 024B-extras CI workflow wiring will hardcode
# --tooling-slice-id per workflow file (cohort workflows omit; tooling workflows
# match). This CLI guard refuses obvious misuse (no slice-id; cohort ID 024C-F);
# residual workflow-side bypass surface is documented + tracked.
```

Add TF-024B-core-WORKFLOW-MITIGATION-001 to STATUS.md tracked-forward (lighter touch than full TF; just a forward-look) noting the workflow-side defense lands at 024B-extras.

