{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-correctness.json && printf '\\\\n---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-safety_bypass.json && printf '\\\\n---\\\\n' && sed -n '1,220p' docs/reviews/WP-SCP-024/024B-core/r1-completeness_governance.json\"","aggregated_output":"{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":733769,\"duration_api_ms\":734690,\"num_turns\":16,\"result\":\"The `.gitignore` has no `tmp.scaffold.*` entry — confirming the pollution risk. Now I have all evidence needed to produce the review JSON.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"sonnet-r1-correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"correctness\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-05-10\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\n  \\\"findings_count\\\": { \\\"CRIT\\\": 0, \\\"MAJ\\\": 2, \\\"MIN\\\": 3, \\\"nit\\\": 1 },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-core correctness posture is substantially sound: bootstrap guard, SHA validation chain, sed-substitution safety (all substituted tokens are validated-safe before sed), template Renovate-marker placement, TF regex construction via re.escape, exactly-one-match assertions on both cascade-status and Target extraction, and the SAFE-003 phantom-entry enforcement all pass inspection. The plan-doc §6 split amendment, D-044 row format, and STATUS.md chain section are present and correctly scoped. Two MAJ defects require a fix round before this slice can advance. MAJ-001 is a silent false-negative bypass on invariant 2: the --diff-base code path in check-invocation-log-entry.sh calls git diff without -C REPO_ROOT, so pathspec BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL (repo-root-relative) resolves relative to cwd instead. Any non-root invocation returns an empty diff, leaving branch_log_modified=0 and allowing onboarded/onboarded-operator-bump cascade-status to pass the log-modification assertion when the log was actually changed. MAJ-002 is a git-workspace pollution risk: test_scaffold_downstream.sh deliberately sets TMPDIR=${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX to work around the macOS system-path denylist, but no .gitignore entry covers tmp.scaffold.* — a SIGKILL leaves these directories in the tracked workspace where a subsequent git add . would ingest them. Three MIN findings cover v1.0.0-vs-v1.0.1 recommendation regression, lowercase-only SHA validation without normalisation, and the scorecard-emit=true coverage being bundled into the master-branch test rather than standing alone. One nit on the V1_0_0_SCP_SHA constant naming.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"git diff in check-invocation-log-entry.sh --diff-base path lacks git -C REPO_ROOT, making pathspec resolution cwd-relative. Invocations from any subdirectory silently return an empty diff, leaving branch_log_modified=0 and bypassing invariant 2 log-modification assertions for onboarded and onboarded-operator-bump.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 239: `git diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\"` — no -C flag. BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL is computed as os.path.relpath(log, REPO_ROOT) so it is repo-root-relative, not cwd-relative. All tests use (cd repo_dir && ...) masking the bug.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Silent false negative: operator running the CLI from a repo subdirectory gets OK on onboarded/onboarded-operator-bump even when branch-protection-log.md was not modified. Directly undermines invariant 2's log-presence assertion — the core audit-trail guarantee.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change line 239 to: `git -C \\\\\\\"$REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\"`. Add a test case that invokes the script from a subdirectory (e.g., scripts/) with an onboarded fixture and verifies it still detects log-not-modified.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-MAJ-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"test_scaffold_downstream.sh sets TMPDIR=${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX to dodge the macOS system-path denylist. No .gitignore entry covers tmp.scaffold.*. If the test process is killed with SIGKILL, the EXIT trap does not fire and the temp root remains in the git workspace, reachable by git add . on next session.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh line 14: `TMPDIR=\\\\\\\"$(mktemp -d \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}/tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"`; grep of .gitignore finds no tmp.scaffold.* entry. DISPATCH-NOTE spec states 'Output dirs created under $TMPDIR (cleaned by EXIT trap, not REPO_ROOT)' — both requirements violated.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"SIGKILL or host power-loss during test leaves tmp.scaffold.XXXXXX/ in git workspace with no gitignore protection. Next `git add .` from an operator or CI pre-commit hook would stage 3-4 generated files (wrapper, CODEOWNERS-snippet, CASCADE-PR-BODY, MANIFEST.json) as tracked content, polluting commits.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add `tmp.scaffold.*` to .gitignore at repo root. Alternatively add `tmp.scaffold.*/` to an existing ignore pattern. The macOS denylist constraint forcing REPO_ROOT usage should also be noted in the DISPATCH-NOTE spec update so the 'not REPO_ROOT' language is corrected to reflect the real posture.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"scaffold-downstream.sh post-v1.2.0 warning recommends V1_0_0_SCP_SHA (v1.0.0 SHA 41a5299) when v1.0.1 is the latest compatible release. Operators following the recommendation forgo the Python supply-chain hardening introduced in v1.0.1 (D-038: pip --require-hashes lockfile).\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh lines 154-158: V1_0_0_SCP_SHA hardcoded to 41a529908ef5355b82ca924ef0502fa5ec2fcc11 (v1.0.0). STATUS.md line 47 confirms v1.0.1 (0e24cc6) was released 2026-05-02 with hash-pinned Python deps (D-038). v1.0.1 < v1.2.0, so it is not affected by the attest-scorecard job ceiling issue (TF-023E-002).\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Cascade adopters who follow the RECOMMENDED guidance get a wrapper pinned to v1.0.0, missing the supply-chain hardening of v1.0.1. Marginal security regression for adopters in the attest-scorecard-blocked scenario.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Set V1_0_0_SCP_SHA (or rename to LAST_SAFE_SCP_SHA) to the v1.0.1 commit SHA (0e24cc6..., need full 40-char). Update the warning text accordingly. Update test SCP_SHA to point at the new constant if changed.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"--scp-sha regex ^[a-f0-9]{40}$ accepts only lowercase hex with no normalization step. Operators who copy a SHA from tooling that emits uppercase (e.g., some git GUIs, GitHub Desktop) receive an exit 1 with a message that says 'lowercase hex' — but the script makes no attempt to lowercase the input before validation.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh line 117: `printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" | grep -qE '^[a-f0-9]{40}$'`. No tr/python lowercase transform precedes this check. Error message 'must be exactly 40 lowercase hex characters' is correct but no suggestion to lowercase is given.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Operator confusion for non-git-output SHA sources. Low frequency but zero-cost to fix.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add `SCP_SHA=\\\\\\\"$(printf '%s' \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" | tr 'A-F' 'a-f')\\\\\\\"` before the regex check, or change the regex to `^[a-fA-F0-9]{40}$` and normalise after acceptance. Update the test to include an uppercase-SHA case that either normalises or rejects with a clear message.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-MIN-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"DISPATCH-NOTE lists scorecard-emit=true as a separate test case; the test exercises it only inside the master-branch case. If the master-branch test is ever refactored to use scorecard-emit=false, the true variant loses coverage with no standalone assertion to catch the regression.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"tests/scaffolder/test_scaffold_downstream.sh lines 268-280: scorecard-emit=true is asserted via grep -Fq 'scorecard-emit: true' within the master-branch variant. DISPATCH-NOTE scope table: 'scorecard-emit=true variant' listed as an independent test case.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Low: coverage exists today. Risk is future refactor silently drops scorecard-emit=true assertion. A standalone test case would make the coverage explicit and regression-resistant.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Extract a dedicated scorecard-emit=true test case using --default-branch main (or develop) + --scorecard-emit true; assert wrapper contains 'scorecard-emit: true' + call validate_manifest with expected_scorecard_emit=true.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-CORR-NIT-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"V1_0_0_SCP_SHA constant is named after the version it currently holds. When the recommended safe SHA changes (e.g., to v1.0.1 or v1.0.2), the version-specific name will mislead readers into thinking the recommendation is still v1.0.0.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh line 16: `V1_0_0_SCP_SHA=\\\\\\\"41a529908ef5355b82ca924ef0502fa5ec2fcc11\\\\\\\"`. The name couples the constant's purpose (last-known-good) to a specific version string.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Documentation/maintainability: future SHA updates require renaming the constant to stay accurate, risking a name-value mismatch if only the value is updated.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Rename to LAST_COMPATIBLE_SCP_SHA or PRE_ATTEST_SCORECARD_SHA with a comment explaining which version it represents and why. Update all references in script + test.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"9b7250b6-f41f-41a9-a184-a34b4102f61c\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.21974655,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":129815,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":414071,\"output_tokens\":40414,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":129815,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":3185,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":134972,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2224,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":2224},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":2397,\"outputTokens\":17,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.002482,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":9,\"outputTokens\":40414,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":414071,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":129815,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.21726455,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"d76a0deb-6498-408b-87c7-5f03f7919aaf\"}\n\n---\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":1081282,\"duration_api_ms\":1082230,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"Now I have all the evidence I need. Let me produce the findings report.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"reviewer\\\": \\\"sonnet-r1-safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"safety_bypass\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024/024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-05-10\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"BLOCKED\\\",\\n  \\\"findings_count\\\": { \\\"CRIT\\\": 1, \\\"MAJ\\\": 1, \\\"MIN\\\": 3, \\\"nit\\\": 1 },\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"The cascade-status spec's exactly-one assertions, regex anchoring, phantom-target-entry guard, bootstrap-only CI check, SHA non-existence exit-2, adopter-slug canonicalisation, template permission ceiling (contents:read + statuses:write only), and fork-PR refusal clause all hold under adversarial scrutiny. The worked-example matrix passes correctly: ex1+ex2 match, ex3-ex6 fail. Multi-occurrence cascade-status injection is closed (Python finds 2 matches, exits 1). Phantom-target-entry attacks are closed (parse_entry_repo exactly-one + em-dash barrier prevents repo-segment injection). Path-traversal bypass on --output-dir is closed: readlink/realpath canonicalise BEFORE the system-path denylist, /private/var/* canonicalisation is correct. D-044 trust-model claims accurately describe the implementation — the slug-collision limitation is faithfully documented. One CRIT defect: `git diff` in --diff-base mode lacks `-C REPO_ROOT`, so the repo-root-relative pathspec resolves against CWD. When invoked from any non-root directory, the branch-protection-log diff is empty regardless of whether the file was actually changed. For `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` this is a silent bypass: branch_log_modified=0, the not-modified assertion passes, and a slice that secretly ran enable-required-check.sh exits 0 with an OK message. One MAJ: `--allow-not-applicable` + any valid `--tooling-slice-id` bypasses all cascade enforcement when DISPATCH-NOTE declares `cascade-status: not applicable`; no machine-detectable slice-type guard prevents a cohort-cascade operator from using this combination. Three MIN findings: CRLF inconsistency in `**Target:**` regex, silent TF-023E-002 warning suppression when v1.2.0 tag is absent, and slug-collision runtime blind-spot. One nit on the pre-readlink `..` pattern check.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-CRIT-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"CRIT\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"`git diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\"` (check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 239) has no `-C` flag. Git resolves pathspecs relative to CWD. From any non-root invocation, the repo-root-relative pathspec finds nothing; diff is empty; branch_log_modified=0. For `blocked-on-adopter-conflict` this silently PASSES the not-modified assertion even when the log was actually changed — a complete bypass of invariant 2's negative audit-trail guard.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 239: `git diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\"` — no `-C \\\\\\\"$REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\"`. BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL is os.path.relpath(log, REPO_ROOT) — repo-root-relative. All tests use `(cd \\\\\\\"$repo_dir\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\"$SCRIPT\\\\\\\" ...)` from the git root, masking the defect. Correctness R1 identified the onboarded false-negative angle; safety angle is the blocked-on-adopter-conflict silent false-positive.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A hostile operator can secretly run enable-required-check.sh (adding a branch-protection-log entry), declare `cascade-status: blocked-on-adopter-conflict`, and invoke the script from a subdirectory. CI exits 0 with 'OK: cascade-status=blocked-on-adopter-conflict; 2 checks passed'. The forensic audit trail has a spurious log entry; the DISPATCH-NOTE falsely records conflict-close. Undetectable without manual log inspection.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change line 239 to: `git -C \\\\\\\"$REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" diff --unified=0 \\\\\\\"${DIFF_BASE}..HEAD\\\\\\\" -- \\\\\\\"$BRANCH_PROTECTION_LOG_REPO_REL\\\\\\\"`. Add a test case that invokes the script from a repo subdirectory with a blocked-on-adopter-conflict fixture where the log WAS modified, and asserts exit 1.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-MAJ-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MAJ\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"`cascade-status: not applicable` in DISPATCH-NOTE combined with `--allow-not-applicable --tooling-slice-id <any-valid-id>` bypasses ALL cascade enforcement — no log check, no target match, no TF reference check. No machine-detectable slice-type guard prevents a cohort-cascade operator (024C/D/E/F) from using this combination. The DISPATCH-NOTE `not applicable` declaration is visible in the PR diff but in single-operator mode (D-031) the operator self-reviews.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh lines 186-195: `not applicable` case exits 0 before target_repo extraction when ALLOW_NOT_APPLICABLE=1 and TOOLING_SLICE_ID ∈ {024A,024B-core,024B-extras,024G}. The guard at lines 201-203 only fires when cascade-status is NOT `not applicable`, so it cannot block the bypass path. DISPATCH-NOTE comment acknowledges: 'Operator responsibility: the slice type is not machine-detectable.'\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A cohort cascade slice can merge with DISPATCH-NOTE declaring `not applicable` and no enforcement of log modification, target match, or TF references. The cascade audit trail is absent for that adopter. Until 024B-extras wires the workflow, the CLI provides no machine barrier.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"In the workflow wiring (024B-extras), hardcode the cohort-slice workflow invocation WITHOUT `--allow-not-applicable`; only the tooling-slice workflow (invoked for 024A/024B-core/024B-extras/024G) passes the flag. Optionally add a DISPATCH-NOTE metadata field (e.g., `slice-type: cohort`) that the script validates against a separate allowlist before accepting `not applicable`.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The `**Target:**` extraction regex uses `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` (space or tab only) while the `cascade-status` extraction regex uses `\\\\\\\\s*$`. On CRLF DISPATCH-NOTEs (Windows checkouts), `[ \\\\\\\\t]` does not match `\\\\\\\\r`, so the `**Target:**` line fails to match → 0 matches → script exits 1 with 'ERROR: expected exactly one **Target:** match, found 0'. Cascade-status extraction works (\\\\\\\\s*$ handles \\\\\\\\r). Systematic false-negative in mixed-platform environments.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh line 212: pattern `r'^[ \\\\\\\\t]*-[ \\\\\\\\t]+\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*Target:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*[ \\\\\\\\t]+([a-zA-Z0-9._/-]+)[ \\\\\\\\t]*$'` — `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` terminal anchor. Compare cascade-status extraction line 165: pattern `r'^cascade-status:\\\\\\\\s+([^\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n]+?)\\\\\\\\s*$'` — `\\\\\\\\s*$` terminal anchor handles \\\\\\\\r. CRLF line `- **Target:** jrnb2024/pim\\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n`: `\\\\\\\\r` is not in `[ \\\\\\\\t]`, regex fails to match.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"On Windows or repos with CRLF line endings, all cascade-status paths that require target extraction (onboarded, onboarded-operator-bump, blocked-on-adopter-conflict) silently fail with a confusing 'found 0 Target matches' error. Not a bypass, but breaks enforcement reliability across platforms.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Change `[ \\\\\\\\t]*$` to `\\\\\\\\s*$` in the **Target:** regex (line 212), matching the cascade-status pattern. Add a test case using a DISPATCH-NOTE with \\\\\\\\r\\\\\\\\n line endings.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"`git merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null` silently fails when the `v1.2.0` tag is absent (shallow clone, fresh local repo). The `if` condition evaluates false; the TF-023E-002 warning is never emitted even for post-v1.2.0 SHAs. Operators scaffolding against a fresh or shallow SCP checkout won't receive the warning and may pin a SHA that triggers the attest-scorecard startup_failure.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh lines 154-158: `if git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" merge-base --is-ancestor v1.2.0 \\\\\\\"$SCP_SHA\\\\\\\" 2>/dev/null; then warn ...`. The `2>/dev/null` swallows the `fatal: Not a valid object name 'v1.2.0'` error. Test at line 169 gates the v1.2.0 warning assertion behind `if git -C \\\\\\\"${REPO_ROOT}\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1` with a SKIP path — confirming the behaviour.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Cascade operators using shallow clones (common in ephemeral CI environments) don't receive the advisory warning about TF-023E-002 even if they're pinning a post-v1.2.0 SHA. Adopters could receive wrappers that cause startup_failure without having been warned by the scaffolder.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Before the `merge-base` call, explicitly check if the tag exists: `if git -C \\\\\\\"$SCP_REPO_ROOT\\\\\\\" rev-parse v1.2.0 >/dev/null 2>&1; then <run merge-base check>; else warn 'cannot confirm SHA lineage: v1.2.0 tag absent; if SHA is post-v1.2.0, TF-023E-002 applies'; fi`.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-MIN-003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Adopter-slug collision (`org/repo-foo` and `org/repo.foo` both canonicalize to `org-repo-foo`) allows one adopter's TF-024X-renovate row in STATUS.md to satisfy the `onboarded-operator-bump` regex check for the other adopter. D-044 documents this as a precondition requirement (unique names post-canonicalization) but no runtime collision detection exists. A future cohort expansion could introduce colliding slugs silently.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/check-invocation-log-entry.sh canonicalize_adopter_slug lines 111-117: `re.sub(r'[^a-z0-9-]+', '-', s.replace('/', '-'))` — `.` and `_` both map to `-`, enabling collision. Python pattern line 296: `rf'TF-024X-renovate-{re.escape(slug)}(?:\\\\\\\\*\\\\\\\\*)? \\\\\\\\((...)'` — re.search matches any occurrence in STATUS.md. Code comment: 'Cohort adopter repo names must remain unique after canonicalization' — documented precondition only.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"If two cohort adopters share a canonicalized slug, a TF-024X-renovate row for adopter A in STATUS.md silently passes the check for adopter B's cascade slice, without adopter B's operator-bump TF being explicitly filed. Threshold A credit could be claimed for adopter B without the required tracking entry. D-044 trust model accurately describes the limitation but no enforcement exists.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a collision-detection step at CLI startup or in the plan-doc operators checklist: before a cascade slice opens, verify `canonicalize_adopter_slug(new_adopter)` does not match any existing STATUS.md TF-024X-renovate slug. Alternatively, append a numeric disambiguator to colliding slugs. Document the collision check in ADOPT-001 §12 per-slice checklist.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"R1-SAFE-NIT-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"The pre-`readlink -f` `..` pattern check (`case \\\\\\\"$OUTPUT_DIR\\\\\\\" in ..|../*|*/../*|*/..)`) fires on the RAW path before canonicalization. The critical system-path denylist correctly fires post-canonicalization. The early check is partially redundant (any `..` traversal that would reach a denied path is already caught by the denylist after readlink) but does reject non-traversal paths containing `..` segments that resolve to safe directories.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh lines 128-132 (early `..` check on raw `$OUTPUT_DIR`) vs lines 143-147 (system-path denylist on canonicalized `$OUTPUT_DIR` after readlink). The early check rejects `~/projects/../pim-clone` even though `readlink -f` would resolve it to a safe non-system path. The denylist at lines 143-147 would catch any path resolving to /etc, /var, /tmp, /private/* regardless.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"No security impact — the denylist is the authoritative guard. The early check is over-restrictive for paths with `..` segments that resolve to safe directories, which may cause user confusion. Defense-in-depth value is minimal since the denylist subsumes the protection.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"No code change required. Optional: add a comment explaining the early `..` check is user-experience hardening (reject confusing paths early) rather than a security boundary, to prevent future maintainers from removing the post-canonicalization denylist assuming the early check is sufficient.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"c41add1a-796b-4e43-abfd-bbd92d691aaf\",\"total_cost_usd\":1.4612747,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":8,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":134582,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":337884,\"output_tokens\":56883,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":134582,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":7304,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":138416,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":3547,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":3547},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":1838,\"outputTokens\":24,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.001958,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6\":{\"inputTokens\":8,\"outputTokens\":56883,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":337884,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":134582,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":1.4593167,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"a1bd232f-532e-490c-9d50-7805da570853\"}\n\n---\n{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":453167,\"duration_api_ms\":453788,\"num_turns\":19,\"result\":\"I now have all the information needed. Let me produce the structured review result.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"review_id\\\": \\\"R1-COMPLETENESS_GOVERNANCE\\\",\\n  \\\"slice\\\": \\\"WP-SCP-024 024B-core\\\",\\n  \\\"lens\\\": \\\"completeness_governance\\\",\\n  \\\"verdict\\\": \\\"APPROVED_WITH_OBSERVATIONS\\\",\\n  \\\"summary\\\": \\\"024B-core is well-governed. The scope-split decision is empirically justified (R6→R7→R8 trajectory) with a clear 'why not descope' framing, audit-trail intact (extras-parking branch), and both carry-forward TFs explicitly named. D-044 is correctly scoped to core deliverables and cites SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md. STATUS.md chain entry covers split decision, outcome rows, extras-parking branch reference, and §6 amendment claim. All five plan-doc invariant 2 check-paths (4 enforcement + not-applicable carve-out) map byte-for-byte to code; exactly-one-match assertions on cascade-status, **Target:**, and log-entry targets close injection-bypass surface. Scaffolder emits 4 files, validates substitutions, enforces system-path denylist including macOS /private/var, warns on post-v1.2.0 SHAs referencing TF-023E-002, and the template carries the Renovate auto-bump marker. Test coverage is thorough (7+ scaffolder cases; all 6 plan-doc worked-example matrix cases; PR-mode + diff-mode paths; slugcanonicalization; phantom-entry SAFE-003). Extras carve-out is clean: no --restore, no break-glass §12.8, no .github/workflows/check-invocation-log-entry.yml. VERSIONING.md alignment correct (all new files are internal tooling; no version bump). Two MIN documentation placement gaps noted below.\\\",\\n  \\\"findings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MIN-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Plan-doc §6 024C acceptance-criteria row does not inline FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 as a gate condition.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"STATUS.md 2026-05-09 PM chain row 3 states '024C entry now conditional on BOTH 024B-core + 024B-extras merged + FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closure'. Checking plan-doc §6 024C table row (line 207): AC column lists 'PIM policy-check live; ≥1 Renovate-bump-propagation cycle merged clean; D-045 filed' — no FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001. Gate appears only in the §6 split-note footnote (line 213), STATUS.md tracked-forward items, and D-044 rationale.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"A slice-024C authoring operator consulting only the §6 table row for its acceptance criteria and prerequisites will not see the FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 gate inline. The footnote and STATUS.md provide the correct signal, but are secondary read paths versus the AC column.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add '024C MUST NOT open until FUP-ACC-INSTALL-TARGET-REPO-001 closes (per STATUS.md TF entry + D-044)' to the 024C row's AC column or deliverables column in plan-doc §6. The footnote and STATUS.md entries remain as supporting context.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-MIN-002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"MIN\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"Plan-doc §5.3 emitted-files bullet list omits MANIFEST.json, creating a drift between the spec text and the implemented behaviour.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"Plan-doc §5.3 lists three emitted files (wrapper, CODEOWNERS-snippet, CASCADE-PR-BODY.md). D-044 rationale and DISPATCH-NOTE scope table both document MANIFEST.json as a fourth emitted file. scaffold-downstream.sh emits it; test_scaffold_downstream.sh validate_manifest asserts len(payload['files']) == 3 (3 non-manifest files) plus MANIFEST.json itself = 4 total. §5.3 bullet list never updated to match.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"Plan-doc §5.3 is the human-readable spec reviewers and future slice authors consult. A reader of §5.3 alone would not expect the MANIFEST.json audit artefact, creating a mismatch between spec text and code surface. Low functional risk since D-044 and tests are authoritative, but §5.3 is a canonical reference.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add '- `<output-dir>/MANIFEST.json` (SHA256 audit manifest per D-044)' to the §5.3 emitted-files bullet list.\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CG-nit-001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"nit\\\",\\n      \\\"claim\\\": \\\"SCOPE-CORRECTION-2026-05-09.md and STATUS.md name R8-CORR-001/002/003 as carry-forward findings by ID but do not enumerate their issue categories inline.\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"SCOPE-CORRECTION §'Carry-forward from extras-parking' says 'informed by R8-CORR-001/002/003 carry-forward findings'; STATUS.md PM chain row 2 says 'R8-CORR-001/002/003 (extras-parking r8-archive) carry forward as known findings.' Neither document lists what defect class each ID represents. The r8-archive directory on the extras-parking branch is the only place to find the content.\\\",\\n      \\\"impact\\\": \\\"The 024B-extras authoring operator must locate and read the r8-archive to understand what the carry-forward findings actually are. Minor operator-UX friction; the branch and archive are correctly preserved and referenced.\\\",\\n      \\\"mitigation\\\": \\\"Add a one-line description per finding ID in SCOPE-CORRECTION §'Carry-forward from extras-parking', e.g. 'R8-CORR-001: <issue class>, R8-CORR-002: <issue class>, R8-CORR-003: <issue class>'. This removes any ambiguity for the extras-authoring operator without requiring archive lookup.\\\"\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"crit_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"maj_count\\\": 0,\\n  \\\"min_count\\\": 2,\\n  \\\"nit_count\\\": 1,\\n  \\\"approved\\\": true\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"a4898